{"number": 21, "text": "                      jump,       next,      glimpsed\n                      Consonant             clusters       at    the    end      of   words\n\n                      There are many  more combinations  of consonant sounds possible at the end of English words\n                      than at the beginning (see Unit 7). There can be up to four consonant sounds in a final\n                      consonant  cluster:\n\n                       Words with...          2 final consonants     3 final consonants      4 final consonants\n                                              honest /st/             helped /Ipt/           prompts /mpts/\n                                              jump /mp/               next /kst/             glimpsed /mpst/\n                                              wrapped /pt/            crisps /sps/           texts /ksts/\n\n                      Some  final clusters with three or four consonants can be difficult to pronounce even for native\n                      English speakers, so in some words these are commonly simplified. For example, the middle\n                      consonant  of the clusters /kts/, /mps/, /mpt/, /nts/, /ndz/ and /skt/ is hardly heard or sometimes\n                      even left out (see also Unit 29A):\n                                                                       jumps —  jumps\n                             products — products /prodaks/\n                                                                       clients — clients /klaron's/\n                            camped   — camped  /keemt/\n                             hands —  hands /hzenz/\n                                                                       asked >  asked /a:st/\n                      Notice also:\n                            twelfth  — twelfth                         fifths + fifths /f10s/ or  /frfs/\n\n                      Leaving final consonants out of consonant clusters at the end of words can cause\n                      misunderstanding, and you  should avoid this. For example, say:\n                            product  (not: product)       jump (not: jump)       hand  (not: hand)\n\n                      In particular, avoid leaving out /z/ or /s/ in plurals and third person singular verb forms, and /t/\n                      or /d/ in -ed verbs and adjectives:\n                                                          sleeps (not: sleeps)\n                            jobs (not: jobs)\n                            laughed  (not: laughed)\n                                                          curved (not: curved)\n\n                      Don’t be tempted to add vowels to consonant  clusters in order to make them easier to say, as\n                      this can also cause misunderstanding. You should —\n                      e avoid adding an extra vowel (usually /1/ or /a/) between consonants:\n                        watched  (not: watch'd)      health (not:                      dogs (not:\n                      e avoid adding an extra vowel (usually /a/ or /u:/) at the end of the word:\n                        last (not: last®)            announce  (not: announce?®)       attempts (not:\n                      e avoid adding an extra vowel at the end of an adjective, as this can sound like a\n                        comparative form:\n                        fast (not: fast® because it sounds like ‘faster’)\n                        damp  (not:       because it sounds like ‘damper’)\n\n                      You  can find more practice of consonant clusters at the end of words in Section E2.\n\n\n             22\n                         English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3638, "mean_conf": 91.62, "word_count": 365, "words": [{"text": "22", "x0": 253, "y0": 3190, "x1": 298, "y1": 3222, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "jump,", "x0": 431, "y0": 108, "x1": 642, "y1": 192, "conf": 91, "bold": false}, {"text": "next,", "x0": 680, "y0": 1